#{[17592186548990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548931 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37056" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548990 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d6/d65013cac950b3e3d533dd6dfbdf1dc6582c10fcb7d80f00ab559935915d9595.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local maximum?\n\nI. $x=-1$\n\nII. $x=1$\n\nIII. $x=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2dea474c-b14e-5037-9ce9-358952e01aab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548990 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function has a local maximum at $x=-1.$ For example, in the interval $(-1.25,-0.75),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=-1,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/a5/a5f0e23d8bed98f693ee72086b0bf17b9f65de8931bba6f16cfcc34e670a757e.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(-1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-1.25,-0.75).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local maximum at $x=1.$ For any open interval containing $x=1,$ there will be points on the graph that lie above $f(1).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local maximum at $x=3.$ For any open interval containing $x=3,$ there will be points on the graph that lie above $f(3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548991 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548915 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548991 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37221" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548991 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548991 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bb/bbe19cf01b4ca415802db1d505b402e2c238aad6c443170cf18cd7b79d7f238a.png)\n\nThe graph of the function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the points shown are the local maxima of the function?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548991 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f37d338-5ba5-5919-bf66-41519f983a1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186548991 #edb/ref 1074 "Consider the point $Q.$ We can take an open interval of $x$-values such that $Q$ will be the highest point on the graph in this interval. Hence, $Q$ is a local maximum.\n\n![](images/14/14d2e4c17f5a79165c2e6b1988fe949625eccb87d2880b1131705028be5884c4.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549004 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549004 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108293" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549004 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549004 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b0/b00882caec033394ff22a01df1f8cc417a8336b475962e73662316c3091229fa.png)\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the function given by the graph above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=-2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549004 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50fb8efe-0b0b-50a4-80a5-fe5eb2cee4f9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549004 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/ed/ed1ad21056cf9c05c621180126387f2e07a1cd349f151726142fb33d2b514e44.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(-2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=1.$ For example, in the interval $(0.5,1.5),$ the value of the function at $x=1$ is the same as the values of the function at the other points of the interval, as shown below. \n\n![](images/ff/ff8960289497889b1fb046c05b0bb2243a8776c435bb685e3c94fb7fe832e056.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(1)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (0.5,1.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local maximum at $x=4.$ For example, in the interval $(3.75,4.25),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=4,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/b2/b2d1f1b6b065c5d3511ec02350eae1a867b9473fa2ac7c1a1cb9458d1421fe03.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(4)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (3.75,4.25).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549033 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549033 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108276" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549033 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549033 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d7/d71ebdb084e23aef4b3901aaba2e4bb4de5438fa41a3fc331bef749f3c78de76.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=-4$\n\nII. $x=-1$\n\nIII. $x=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549033 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95d717c9-1435-5503-b9b6-aa0cf5a741dc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549033 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=-4.$ For any open interval containing $x=-4$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(-4).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=-1.$ For any open interval containing $x=-1$, there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(-1).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/bf/bfe274fe02b7200164eaad5c98aa2b19297738987f70059157d2895b989a962c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549035 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548869 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549035 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108281" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549035 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549035 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b3/b39c4e174ccb11a533586b57f26958a8ebfb5de0f0920a86297653471862fa7f.png)\n\nLet $f(x)$ be the function shown above. Which of the following statements are true? \n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=-3$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=0$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549035 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a017d206-18fc-5343-8dce-b59fb9d7e098" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549035 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=-3.$ For example, in the interval $(-3.5,-2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/be/be3b86690beb961d54380b72736d945af3d735410da3bfe34cfb2cdead859f81.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(-3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-3.5,-2.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=0.$ For example, in the interval $(-0.5,0.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=0,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/43/439117bd94dbc147d3cd2de49abd45c4b4790251ce25d1a79ecb31e03081068c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(0)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-0.5,0.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.75,3.25),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/33/33aee4724507cff3e9ca87c78aa77c0ecb0df5f59a7bcf77359c1ca4e957f73c.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(3)\\le f(x)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.75,3.25).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549060 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549060 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90634" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549060 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549060 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/d1/d13474a62324af5edcaafd10e60c6b02c257f32c015d5769512725d157e01420.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=2$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=5$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549060 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e60840d8-8ef2-5027-842a-3130fbad5883" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549060 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greater than $f(4).$ For example, $f(7)>f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/32/32c866706140c0bc9a40bcb0ffdca4a827578e2d16dbcefc2cdb66b5c05b1c1c.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function does *not* have a global minimum at $x=5.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is smaller than $f(5).$ For example, $f(0)<f(5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549062 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548946 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549062 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108273" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549062 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549062 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/69/69217ab9fc86fdbb424a0716f00596197b7da9e930c74b12b82406ce2ee8354c.png)\n\nFor which of the following values of $x$ does the function $y=f(x),$ shown above, attain a local minimum?\n\nI. $x=-2$\n\nII. $x=2$\n\nIII. $x=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549062 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ea412ed1-a3cb-5bcc-876d-3ca4d60883c9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549062 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given points in turn.\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For any open interval containing $x=-2,$ there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(-2).$\n\n- The function has a local minimum at $x=2.$ For example, in the interval $(1.5,2.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/8e/8e56e79caf5c7903de977ee66894961b6068e3974ffd9ff4482b2086fad290ef.png)\n\nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (1.5,2.5).$\n\n- The function does *not* have a local minimum at $x=4.$ For any open interval containing $x=4,$ there will be points on the graph that lie below $f(4).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549066 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549066 #edb/ref 1069 "q-108296" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549066 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549066 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e0/e0b8b8968b650e53d35766ede2b22aa187a7301f559d1687fa6bc630c1152701.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=-2$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** minimum at $x=1$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a local maximum at $x=3$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549066 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f3f7c8b0-18ee-5d7c-bcd5-63f6de1aef9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549066 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=-2.$ For example, in the interval $(-2.5,-1.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=-2,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/f7/f7abc15ac37d26d6daea9855cdebd2ef0ae5e27a27d65480b51da3cb7b19a395.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(-2)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (-2.5,-1.5).$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, the function has a global minimum at $x=1.$ The global minimum is the lowest point on the graph and our function does not have points where its value is smaller than $f(1).$\n\n- Statement III is true. The function has a local maximum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the greatest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/19/19d3370f7113e6ebfaa2d1f53a3d097de796e6d3413f06414d35e6a7ade9b9b6.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\le f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549068 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186548907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549068 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549068 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549068 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8e/8e0125007c4384cdd3eb5d939176b2d1ede7a6525da26fb5e99ccfe67e4077a9.png)\n\nThe graph of a function $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=4$\n\nII. $f(x)$ attains a local minimum at $x=3$\n\nIII. $f(x)$ attains a **global** maximum at $x=1$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549068 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f8983cc1-bd12-59ce-89e8-0458cbfae2b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549068 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, the function has a global maximum at $x=4.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph and our function does *not* have points where its value is greater than $f(4).$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function has a local minimum at $x=3.$ For example, in the interval $(2.5,3.5),$ the smallest value of the function is attained at $x=3,$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/c2/c288ece1ca29e5da8ec8d64583b25f7538a929a3de295c24be6396339649d2d4.png)\n\n \nWe can write this mathematically as $f(x)\\ge f(3)\\,$ for all $\\,x\\in (2.5,3.5).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function does *not* have a global maximum at $x=1.$ The global maximum is the highest point on the graph, but our function has points where its value is greatest than $f(1).$ For example, $f(4)>f(1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
